":\"wROQUZDCIMI\",\"title\":\"Why Are Emulators Legal? Dolphin vs. Nintendo, and the Fate of Emulation [Great Emu War Pt. 1]\",\"channel\":\"Moon Channel\",\"summary\":\"Moon Channel, run by a lawyer, explains the legal basis for emulation, including the history of emulation case law and Nintendo's takedown of Dolphin's Steam release. It argues the legality of emulators is more fragile than most people assume.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a clear, lawyer-made summary during Nintendo emulator disputes like Yuzu.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"emulator legality\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Game history & retro gaming\"],\"tags\":[\"emulation\",\"dolphin\",\"nintendo\",\"dmca\",\"yuzu\",\"copyright law\"],\"quote\":\"I really recommend folks watch this, from a lawyer\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"wfzSE4Hoxbc\",\"title\":\"David Deutsch on Physics Without Probability\",\"channel\":\"Simon Benjamin\",\"summary\":\"A 2015 talk by David Deutsch with slides and audio about constructor theory and how physics, in particular the many-worlds interpretation, can be framed without probability.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads on probability and quantum theory as a strong argument that probabilities can be eliminated from physics.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"physics without probability\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum physics\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"david deutsch\",\"many-worlds\",\"constructor theory\",\"probability\",\"quantum mechanics\"],\"quote\":\"Worth a watch especially if you balk at this idea just to to see a strong counter argument.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"wq5D43qAsVg\",\"title\":\"Everything is a Remix Part 3 (Original Series, 2011)\",\"channel\":\"Kirby Ferguson\",\"summary\":\"Part 3 of Kirby Ferguson's Everything is a Remix series, using Apple and the iPhone to show how invention builds on copying and combining prior ideas. It argues that creativity and innovation are inseparable from copying.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in copyright and patent debates to argue that creative works and inventions evolve by building on earlier ones.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"creativity and copying\",\"categories\":[\"Documentaries & storytelling\",\"Product & industrial design\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"remix\",\"copyright\",\"innovation\",\"apple\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"Then watch this rather insightful video titled 'Everything is a Remix'\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xGbgDf4HCHU\",\"title\":\"Sir Roger Penrose & Dr. Stuart Hameroff: CONSCIOUSNESS AND THE PHYSICS OF THE BRAIN\",\"channel\":\"The Qualcomm Institute\",\"summary\":\"A 2020 UC San Diego event with Roger Penrose and Stuart Hameroff presenting their Orch OR theory that consciousness arises from quantum processes in brain microtubules, followed by a conversation with Roger Bingham.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as a presentation of the Penrose-Hameroff theory of consciousness, which some find compelling.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum theories of consciousness\",\"categories\":[\"Consciousness & cognition\",\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"consciousness\",\"penrose\",\"hameroff\",\"microtubules\",\"orch or\",\"quantum biology\"],\"quote\":\"for the first time I think a truly compelling idea of what consciousness is in a scientific way that could potentially be empirically measured.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xL_sMXfzzyA\",\"title\":\"A Conversation with Bertrand Russell (1952)\",\"channel\":\"Manufacturing Intellect\",\"summary\":\"A 1952 filmed interview in which Romney Wheeler talks with philosopher Bertrand Russell at his home in Surrey. Russell discusses his outlook and reflects on how the world changed over the first half of the century.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users share it as a chance to hear Russell speak himself and get a sense of his values and outlook.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bertrand russell\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\"],\"tags\":[\"philosophy\",\"russell\",\"archival interview\",\"1952\"],\"quote\":\"This is a wonderful interview with him that gives a great sense of what he was all about\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xMabpBvtXr4\",\"title\":\"The Alt-Right Playbook: The Card Says Moops\",\"channel\":\"Innuendo Studios\",\"summary\":\"An Innuendo Studios video essay from the Alt-Right Playbook series that uses the Seinfeld 'the card says moops' scene to explain bad-faith argument tactics. It describes how reactionaries use sincerity and irony as shields in online debate.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend the series for understanding bad-faith 'just kidding' dynamics and trolling online.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bad-faith online argument\",\"categories\":[\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Social issues & culture\"],\"tags\":[\"alt-right\",\"trolling\",\"video essay\",\"internet culture\",\"rhetoric\"],\"quote\":\"I can only recommend watching Innuendo Studios' _Alt-Right Playbook_ series for a deeper understanding of the dynamics\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xR7XMkjDGw0\",\"title\":\"George RR Martin asks Stephen King: \\\"How do you write so Fast?!\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Aegon Targaryen\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of George R.R. Martin asking Stephen King on stage how he writes so fast. King describes setting daily page goals, around six pages a day, and keeping focused hours.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared in productivity and writing threads for King's simple daily-quota approach to producing a lot of work.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"writing productivity\",\"categories\":[\"Literature & writing craft\",\"Productivity & self-improvement\"],\"tags\":[\"stephen king\",\"george rr martin\",\"writing habits\",\"daily routine\",\"productivity\"],\"quote\":\"One of my favorite videos is when George RR Martin asks Stephen King on stage\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xUezfuy8Qpc\",\"title\":\"Rowan Atkinson on free speech\",\"channel\":\"The Christian Institute\",\"summary\":\"Rowan Atkinson speaks against the word 'insulting' in the UK Public Order Act, as part of the Reform Section 5 campaign. He argues for robust free speech and against the slippery slope of criminalizing offense.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters point to it as an eloquent defense of free speech with concrete examples of the slippery slope.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"free speech\",\"categories\":[\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\",\"Law, policing & justice\"],\"tags\":[\"rowan atkinson\",\"free speech\",\"public order act\",\"censorship\",\"uk law\"],\"quote\":\"Rowan Atkinson explained it more eloquently that I ever could\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"xY45YE7ggng\",\"title\":\"The Charming Genius of the Apollo Guidance Computer - Brian Troutwine\",\"channel\":\"Erlang Solutions\",\"summary\":\"Brian Troutwine's Code Mesh 2015 talk on the Apollo Guidance Computer. It covers the AGC's design, core rope memory, its interrupt and software behavior, and the pioneering engineering effort behind it.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a wonderful overview of the AGC architecture, though one commenter found it uninspiring.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"apollo guidance computer\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Space exploration & spaceflight\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"apollo\",\"agc\",\"core rope memory\",\"space computing\",\"history of computing\"],\"quote\":\"The Charming Genius of the Apollo Guidance Computer\\\" is a wonderful talk\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xYemnKEKx0c\",\"title\":\"Strange answers to the psychopath test | Jon Ronson | TED\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"Jon Ronson, author of The Psychopath Test, gives a TED talk on the blurry line between sane and crazy and on psychopathy as a spectrum, with stories of how the label gets applied.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as one of the great non-technical talks, appearing in best-talk lists.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"psychopathy and mental health\",\"categories\":[\"Mental health & neurodiversity\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"jon ronson\",\"psychopathy\",\"psychology\",\"ted talk\",\"mental illness\"],\"quote\":\"In my opinion it is one of the greatest talks of all time and it gave me goosebumps.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"x_q50tvbQm4\",\"title\":\"Susan Kare explains Macintosh UI ergonomics on the Computer Chronicles (1984)\",\"channel\":\"erg0steur\",\"summary\":\"A 1984 Computer Chronicles segment in which Susan Kare explains the ergonomics and interface design of the original Macintosh. She introduces the interface step by step for newcomers.\",\"why_hn\":\"Praised as a lesson in explaining technology to non-technical people and as a window into early Mac design.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"macintosh interface design\",\"categories\":[\"GUI, HCI & interface history\",\"UX & UI design\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"susan kare\",\"macintosh\",\"ui design\",\"computer chronicles\",\"history of computing\"],\"quote\":\"I think some of us (me included) would do well to keep this video in mind when explaining technology to non-technical people.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xb8idEf-Iak\",\"title\":\"1993 interview re: Paul Rand and Steve Jobs\",\"channel\":\"youareworthy\",\"summary\":\"A 1993 interview in which Steve Jobs talks about designer Paul Rand, who created the NeXT logo. He recounts Rand's insistence on presenting a single design and his uncompromising professionalism.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters share it for Jobs's account of Rand's single-option approach and as a lesson in trusting designers.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"design and branding\",\"categories\":[\"Graphic design & typography\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Product & industrial design\"],\"tags\":[\"steve jobs\",\"paul rand\",\"logo design\",\"next\",\"branding\"],\"quote\":\"I really enjoyed his uncompromising attitude\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"xcwJt4bcnXs\",\"title\":\"Stadiums: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)\",\"channel\":\"LastWeekTonight\",\"summary\":\"John Oliver's Last Week Tonight segment on cities spending public money on privately owned sports stadiums, including how tax-exempt municipal bonds subsidize them.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to in debates over stadium subsidies as a persuasive case that cities should not pay for stadiums.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"stadium subsidies\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Politics & history (other)\",\"Satire & parody\"],\"tags\":[\"public funding\",\"stadiums\",\"municipal bonds\",\"john oliver\",\"nfl\"],\"quote\":\"After watching it, it's pretty clear that cities should not pay for stadiums.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"y5UT04p5f7U\",\"title\":\"Death to Pennies\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey's short animated argument for abolishing the US penny, covering its cost and history.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as the canonical concise case against pennies whenever coin abolition comes up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"abolishing the penny\",\"categories\":[\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\"],\"tags\":[\"pennies\",\"currency\",\"economics\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"CGP Grey has an excellent youtube video on the whole issue for the US.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"yYTSdlOdkn0\",\"title\":\"Decaf Explained\",\"channel\":\"James Hoffmann\",\"summary\":\"James Hoffmann explains how decaf coffee is made, how the decaffeination processes differ, and why decaf is roasted and stales differently. He also discusses decaf quality and health questions.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a well-researched overview of decaf processes and quality, and cite it for the claim that decaf stales faster.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"decaf coffee\",\"categories\":[\"Cooking & food\"],\"tags\":[\"decaffeination\",\"coffee roasting\",\"james hoffmann\",\"caffeine\"],\"quote\":\"his video about decaf sold me on the idea\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"yqMjMPlXzdA\",\"title\":\"BUSTED: The Citizen's Guide to Surviving Police Encounters\",\"channel\":\"Flex Your Rights\",\"summary\":\"A 45-minute Flex Your Rights documentary on how to deal with police in three situations: traffic stops, encounters at home, and on the street. It presents practical advice on asserting constitutional rights and refusing searches.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a practical guide to knowing your rights during police encounters, alongside the well-known advice to not talk to the cops.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"rights in police encounters\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"police\",\"civil rights\",\"traffic stops\",\"searches\",\"legal advice\"],\"quote\":\"Excellent 45 minute ACLU documentary on how to interact with police in three situations: traffic stop, in your house, and on foot.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"ywWBy6J5gz8\",\"title\":\"Quick-sort with Hungarian (Küküllőmenti legényes) folk dance\",\"channel\":\"AlgoRythmics\",\"summary\":\"Hungarian folk dancers from Sapientia University act out the quicksort algorithm step by step, with music. It is part of the AlgoRythmics series of sorting algorithms performed as dance.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a memorable and fun way to visualize how sorting algorithms work.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"sorting algorithm visualization\",\"categories\":[\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"quicksort\",\"folk dance\",\"algorithms\",\"algorythmics\",\"teaching\"],\"quote\":\"my very favorite visualizations of sorting algorithms are the Hungarian dancers\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"zDEpeWQHtFU\",\"title\":\"Glenn Vanderburg of LivingSocial on why software development is an engineering discipline\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"Glenn Vanderburg argues at the O'Reilly Software Architecture Conference that software development is an engineering discipline, even if immature. He contends the discredited body of knowledge called software engineering has led people to wrongly reject the label.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended in debates over whether software is real engineering, as a well-argued case that building is cheap relative to simulating.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"software engineering as engineering\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"software engineering\",\"engineering discipline\",\"software architecture\",\"glenn vanderburg\"],\"quote\":\"Required viewing\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"zaXKQ70q4KQ\",\"title\":\"The Man Who Took LSD and Changed The World\",\"channel\":\"Veritasium\",\"summary\":\"Veritasium tells the story of Kary Mullis, the surfer and LSD user who invented PCR and won a Nobel Prize. It explains how PCR works and how it was developed at Cetus.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as one of the most accessible introductions to PCR and its history.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of pcr\",\"categories\":[\"Biology & biotechnology\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"pcr\",\"kary mullis\",\"dna\",\"taq polymerase\",\"nobel prize\"],\"quote\":\"It's one of the most accessible videos on PCR I've ever come across\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"zyQwAhppWj8\",\"title\":\"COLLAPSE of Personal Computing | Investigation Into the Destruction of Ownership\",\"channel\":\"Gamers Nexus\",\"summary\":\"A roughly 3.5-hour Gamers Nexus investigation into rising PC component prices, driven largely by AI data-center demand for memory and other hardware. It draws on interviews with industry figures to argue that personal computing and ownership are being eroded.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a documentary on the hardware shortage told from the perspective of consumer-facing companies, and cited in threads on RAM prices and ownership.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"pc hardware price crisis\",\"categories\":[\"Consumer devices & gadgets\",\"Tech industry & business models\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"ai data centers\",\"memory prices\",\"pc building\",\"ownership\",\"hardware industry\"],\"quote\":\"GN did a documentary on the situation from the perspective of consumer-facing companies.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.5},{\"vid\":\"-7qT_38iRSc\",\"title\":\"Stanford Seminar - Unethical Algorithms of Massive Scale\",\"channel\":\"Stanford Online\",\"summary\":\"Psychologist Robert Epstein presents a Stanford EE380 colloquium on how internet algorithms such as search rankings can manipulate opinions, purchases and votes at massive scale. He also describes a new monitoring system for tracking such influence.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in threads about search bias, election influence and big-tech data practices as the most comprehensive video of Epstein's research.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"algorithmic manipulation\",\"categories\":[\"Privacy, surveillance & cybercrime\",\"AI & data (other)\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"search engine manipulation\",\"elections\",\"tech ethics\",\"online influence\",\"monitoring\"],\"quote\":\"It's worth watching even if it's 1 hour and 23 minutes.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"-C-JoyNuQJs\",\"title\":\"Douglas Crockford: The JSON Saga\",\"channel\":\"YUI Library\",\"summary\":\"Douglas Crockford tells how JSON was discovered rather than invented, and how its simplicity made it a dominant data interchange format. He also covers surrounding history such as earlier Netscape usage and the 'Good, not Evil' license story.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as Crockford's first-hand account of JSON's origins and of the no-evil license anecdote.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"history of JSON\",\"categories\":[\"Web development\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Software practice, ope