 his attempt to build a toaster from scratch, from mining ore and deriving plastic from oil. It is a short TED talk on how much civilization a simple object depends on.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the standard illustration of hidden complexity and supply-chain dependencies behind everyday objects.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"building from scratch\",\"categories\":[\"Manufacturing & industrial processes\",\"Product & industrial design\"],\"tags\":[\"toaster project\",\"manufacturing\",\"supply chains\",\"design\",\"ted talk\"],\"quote\":\"a great example of the hidden complexities of\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"5P5Mk_IggE0\",\"title\":\"Oxide and Friends 7/3/2023 -- Shipping the first Oxide rack: Your questions answered!\",\"channel\":\"Oxide Computer Company\",\"summary\":\"A two-hour Oxide and Friends session answering questions after shipping the first Oxide rack. Topics include making hardware as a startup and why Oxide chose illumos.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as Oxide's own explanation of how they ship hardware as a startup and why they picked illumos.\",\"content_type\":\"podcast\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"hardware startup\",\"categories\":[\"Startups & entrepreneurship\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\",\"Operating systems & kernels\"],\"tags\":[\"oxide computer\",\"illumos\",\"rack-scale servers\",\"hardware startups\",\"q&a\"],\"quote\":\"We have talked quite a bit about what it's like to be making hardware as a startup\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"5V1ynVyud4M\",\"title\":\"\\\"Eve\\\" by Chris Granger\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Chris Granger presents Eve, a programming environment designed to be approachable for humans, at Strange Loop 2015. The talk covers the project's approach and design ideas.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN mentions it as one of the talks on Eve, usually as related prior work in discussions of end-user and visual programming.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming environments\",\"categories\":[\"Programming language design\",\"GUI, HCI & interface history\"],\"tags\":[\"eve\",\"chris granger\",\"strange loop\",\"end-user programming\",\"bret victor\"],\"quote\":null,\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"5ZjhNTM8XU8\",\"title\":\"\\\"Transactions: myths, surprises and opportunities\\\" by Martin Kleppmann\",\"channel\":\"Strange Loop Conference\",\"summary\":\"Martin Kleppmann's Strange Loop talk on database transactions, covering why they were dropped by NoSQL, their reputation for being slow, and newer algorithms that make serializable transactions perform and scale. It touches on consistency, linearizability and the CAP theorem.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a compact preview of Kleppmann's data systems material, including from his book on the subject.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"database transactions\",\"categories\":[\"Databases & storage\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"transactions\",\"consistency\",\"linearizability\",\"acid\",\"distributed systems\",\"kleppmann\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend watching this talk from him to give a little glimpse of the book\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"5_-Ac68FKG4\",\"title\":\"What Eating the Rich Did For Japan\",\"channel\":\"Asianometry\",\"summary\":\"Asianometry traces how the Japanese zaibatsu families grew into vast industrial combines and how postwar reforms abruptly broke up their wealth and control.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a look at how Japan's monopolies were dismantled and how that shaped later industry.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"japanese zaibatsu\",\"categories\":[\"World history\",\"Macroeconomics & financial crises\",\"Labor, work & inequality\"],\"tags\":[\"zaibatsu\",\"japan economic history\",\"monopolies\",\"wealth inequality\",\"postwar reform\"],