puter graphics & rendering\",\"Game programming & engines\",\"Creative coding & generative art\"],\"tags\":[\"fractals\",\"ray marching\",\"procedural rendering\",\"physics engine\",\"game demo\"],\"quote\":\"He has a YouTube channel as well\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9WfZuNceFDM\",\"title\":\"Sassy Justice with Fred Sassy (Full Episode) | Deep Fake and Deep Fake: The Movie\",\"channel\":\"Sassy Justice\",\"summary\":\"Full episode of Sassy Justice, a satirical local-news show from the South Park creators starring a deepfaked consumer advocate, Fred Sassy. It uses face-swapped public figures to comment on the dangers of manipulated video.\",\"why_hn\":\"Brought up in every deepfake thread as a funny and technically convincing early example of synthetic media used for satire.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"deepfakes\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"LLMs & generative AI\",\"Media, journalism & propaganda\"],\"tags\":[\"deepfake\",\"satire\",\"south park\",\"synthetic media\",\"parody\"],\"quote\":\"A hilarious take on deepfakes\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9X68dm92HVI\",\"title\":\"Are we in control of our decisions? | Dan Ariely\",\"channel\":\"TED\",\"summary\":\"In a TED talk, behavioral economist Dan Ariely uses visual illusions and his research to show that people are less rational in their decisions than they think. He covers defaults, such as organ donation rates, and how choice framing steers decisions.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used to illustrate predictable irrationality, such as defaults and the effect of the middle option among three choices.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"behavioral economics\",\"categories\":[\"Microeconomics & behavioral economics\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"dan ariely\",\"irrationality\",\"defaults\",\"decision making\",\"ted\"],\"quote\":\"talks about the very significant differences in organ donation rates between countries\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9XkHBmE-N34\",\"title\":\"The Black Hole Information Paradox\",\"channel\":\"PBS Space Time\",\"summary\":\"PBS Space Time episode on the black hole information paradox, covering how black holes seem to destroy information, the no-hair conjecture and the proposed role of the event horizon. It is part of a series on black holes.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of how information may be encoded on a black hole's surface.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"black hole information paradox\",\"categories\":[\"Cosmology & astrophysics\",\"Quantum physics\"],\"tags\":[\"event horizon\",\"no-hair theorem\",\"holographic principle\",\"black holes\",\"pbs space time\"],\"quote\":\"There's a great PBS Space Time youtube video on this concept\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9ZX_XCYokQo\",\"title\":\"Glenn Gould and Leonard Bernstein: Bach's Keyboard Concerto No. 1 (I) in D minor (BWV 1052)\",\"channel\":\"AntPDC\",\"summary\":\"A 1960 CBS clip in which Leonard Bernstein introduces and conducts Bach's Keyboard Concerto No. 1 in D minor with a 28-year-old Glenn Gould at the piano. Gould's performance starts around 5:08.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a definitive Gould interpretation whose playing makes Bach's counterpoint clear.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"bach keyboard concertos\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"bach\",\"glenn gould\",\"bernstein\",\"piano\",\"counterpoint\"],\"quote\":\"Have a look/listen at Glenn Gould's version, which makes the counterpoint really clear [1].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9dKiLclupUM\",\"title\":\"Naive empiricism, terrorism & Ebola.\",\"channel\":\"N N Taleb's Probability Questions \",\"summary\":\"Nassim Taleb argues that evidence on terrorism risk presented in media is statistically naive, using extreme value theory without math to separate fat-tailed risks like Ebola and terrorism from thin-tailed ones.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it when arguing that different risks follow different distributions and should be judged by growth rates and tails rather than current counts.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"fat-tailed risk\",\"categories\":[\"Probability & game theory\",\"Data science & statistics\"],\"tags\":[\"nassim taleb\",\"extreme value theory\",\"terrorism\",\"risk\",\"black swan\"],\"quote\":\"Watch this to understand that we're talking about two different distributions\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"9i1WlcCudpU\",\"title\":\"10 Reasons to Ignore AI Safety\",\"channel\":\"Robert Miles AI Safety \",\"summary\":\"Robert Miles goes through ten common reasons people give for dismissing AI safety, adapted from Stuart Russell's list, and answers each one. It is aimed at skeptics and newcomers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters link it as a compact rebuttal to dismissive arguments and as a way to see why people consider AI safety important.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intro\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai safety\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\"],\"tags\":[\"ai risk\",\"agi\",\"stuart russell\",\"robert miles\",\"alignment\"],\"quote\":\"This video might be interesting to see some arguments for why people think it’s important\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0}",
