",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LKiBlGDfRU8\",\"title\":\"My dream died, and now I'm here\",\"channel\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder\",\"summary\":\"Sabine Hossenfelder explains why she left academia, criticizing its incentives, funding and career structure. She gives a personal account of the social problems in science.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN users cite it in threads about academia, grant incentives, fraud and women-only programs, often as an eye-opening personal take.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"problems in academia\",\"categories\":[\"Scientific method & science communication\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"academia\",\"science funding\",\"physics\",\"career\",\"sabine hossenfelder\"],\"quote\":\"this video[0] from Sabine Hossenfelder about academics in general is eye opening.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LKp2gikIkD8\",\"title\":\"Weird Kids' Videos and Gaming the Algorithm\",\"channel\":\"Folding Ideas\",\"summary\":\"Folding Ideas examines weird, algorithm-gamed children's content on YouTube, from automated toddler videos and educational spam to exploitative prank channels. It looks at how autoplay and recommendation systems reward this material.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN people cite it as an eye-opening summary of Elsagate and the manipulation of YouTube's algorithm in threads about bot-made and kids' content.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"youtube kids content exploitation\",\"categories\":[\"Media, journalism & propaganda\",\"Internet culture & memes\",\"Futurism & technology and society\"],\"tags\":[\"elsagate\",\"youtube algorithm\",\"children's content\",\"autoplay\",\"video essay\"],\"quote\":\"Bit of a lengthy watch, but this video is pretty eye-opening\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LLCF7vPanrY\",\"title\":\"A Time-Lapse Map of Every Nuclear Explosion Since 1945 - by  Isao Hashimoto\",\"channel\":\"aConcernedHuman\",\"summary\":\"Isao Hashimoto's time-lapse map shows all 2053 nuclear explosions between 1945 and 1998, with each nation getting a blip and a running tally. Each second represents one month.\",\"why_hn\":\"Used as a striking visualization of the sheer scale and rhythm of nuclear testing, and as a model for conveying scale with a counter.\",\"content_type\":\"other\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"nuclear testing history\",\"categories\":[\"Data visualization & information design\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"nuclear weapons\",\"time-lapse\",\"data visualization\",\"cold war\",\"art\"],\"quote\":\"It really puts into perspective just how many nukes have gone off for purposes other than to kill people.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LMxate9gegg\",\"title\":\"The Secret Life of the Radio - Remastered\",\"channel\":\"tim hunkin\",\"summary\":\"Remastered episode of Tim Hunkin's The Secret Life of Machines about the radio, covering its development including Hertz and Marconi experiments and Armstrong's FM radio in animated form.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends the series as awesome and notes it is now legally on YouTube; commenters cite it for radio history.\",\"content_type\":\"documentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"radio technology history\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"radio\",\"tim hunkin\",\"marconi\",\"fm radio\",\"how machines work\"],\"quote\":\"awesome series, now freely/legally posted on YT\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LNjJTgXujno\",\"title\":\"Peter Norvig at Startup School 08\",\"channel\":\"startupschool\",\"summary\":\"Peter Norvig, then Director of Research at Google, talks at Startup School 2008 about leveraging large amounts of data and about technical challenges in Google projects such as image search. A central idea is that data can beat hand-written programming.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a source for Norvig's 'unreasonable effectiveness of data' argument and for his views on image search and the semantic web.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"data-driven machine learning\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"Startups & entrepreneurship\"],\"tags\":[\"peter norvig\",\"google\",\"image search\",\"startup school\",\"big data\",\"semantic web\"],\"quote\":\"the startup school video that quote comes from is worth watching\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LPqtfC70QTU\",\"title\":\"Apple Watch : Scientific Sleep Test\",\"channel\":\"The Quantified Scientist\",\"summary\":\"The Quantified Scientist, a sleep researcher, tests Apple Watch sleep tracking against a research-grade Dreem 2 headband, comparing accuracy for sleep stages, deep and REM sleep. The review is data-focused rather than feature-focused.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters cite it as evidence that Apple Watch is the most accurate consumer sleep tracker and trust the channel for its scientific approach.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sleep tracker accuracy\",\"categories\":[\"Fitness, sleep & longevity\",\"Consumer devices & gadgets\"],\"tags\":[\"apple watch\",\"sleep tracking\",\"wearables\",\"dreem headband\",\"device review\"],\"quote\":\"He’s a professional scientist specialising in sleep research and the reviews concentrate on data/accuracy only.\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LZPKTaVB1IU\",\"title\":\"Debunking Dr. Robert Lustig's Claims from The Huberman Lab Podcast | Educational Video | Biolayne\",\"channel\":\"Dr. Layne Norton\",\"summary\":\"Dr. Layne Norton critiques claims made by Dr. Robert Lustig on the Huberman Lab podcast, going through them against the nutrition research with cited sources. It covers topics like sugar, calories and fruit juice.\",\"why_hn\":\"Linked as a rebuttal when Lustig's or similar influencer nutrition claims come up.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"nutrition science debunking\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"nutrition\",\"robert lustig\",\"huberman lab\",\"sugar\",\"fact-checking\"],\"quote\":\"Here's Layne Norton's response to Lustig and that exact Huberman episode\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LabqeJEOQyI\",\"title\":\"Web 2.0 Expo NY: Clay Shirky (shirky.com) It's Not Information Overload. It's Filter Failure.\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"Clay Shirky argues at Web 2.0 Expo NY that the problem online is not information overload but filter failure. He says the real challenge is building better ways to filter and organize what we see.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN invokes it as a reframing of the information overload problem, often when discussing content filtering, tagging and curation.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"information filtering\",\"categories\":[\"Futurism & technology and society\",\"Internet culture & memes\"],\"tags\":[\"clay shirky\",\"filter failure\",\"information overload\",\"curation\",\"social web\"],\"quote\":\"Ted has a knack for reframing things in ways that make the arbitrariness of certain historical decisions clear\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LdOe18KhtT4\",\"title\":\"Velocity 09:  John Allspaw and Paul Hammond, \\\"10+ Deploys Pe\",\"channel\":\"O'Reilly\",\"summary\":\"John Allspaw and Paul Hammond's Velocity 09 talk on how Flickr achieved ten or more deploys per day through close cooperation between development and operations.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN regards it as a pivotal early talk in the DevOps movement and the original source for the idea.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"devops\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"continuous deployment\",\"flickr\",\"dev and ops\",\"velocity conference\",\"release engineering\"],\"quote\":\"It was really a pivotal talk in the early days of the DevOps movement\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"Lg_aUOSLuRo\",\"title\":\"Defeating Microwave Weapons! - Part 1\",\"channel\":\"Tech Ingredients\",\"summary\":\"Tech Ingredients demonstrates how microwaves work and how they affect objects at range, then shows ways to defend against them. It includes a correction about rectifier output frequency.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recommend it as a practical explanation of microwave hazards and shielding, and of using microwave ovens safely without an enclosure.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"microwave radiation and shielding\",\"categories\":[\"Electronics & circuit design\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\",\"Military history & warfare\"],\"tags\":[\"microwaves\",\"rf energy\",\"shielding\",\"directed energy\",\"electronics\"],\"quote\":\"made an instructional video about how microwaves work and how to defend against them\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LmlAYnFF_s8\",\"title\":\"High speed camera reveals why sodium explodes!\",\"channel\":\"Thunderf00t\",\"summary\":\"Thunderf00t presents high-speed camera footage of alkali metals such as sodium and NaK reacting with water. The footage shows that the explosion is not caused by hydrogen ignition, as the usual explanation says. It is a Coulomb explosion driven by electrons leaving the metal.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as the accessible video explanation of the sodium-in-water research, and when Coulomb explosions or sodium batteries come up.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"alkali metal explosions\",\"categories\":[\"Chemistry\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"sodium\",\"coulomb explosion\",\"high-speed camera\",\"chemistry\",\"water reactions\"],\"quote\":\"the video has a nice balance, it's technical but not too technical\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"LtlrITxB5qg\",\"title\":\"The IBM 1401 mainframe runs \\\"Edith\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CuriousMarc\",\"summary\":\"CuriousMarc runs the program 'Edith' on a 1959 IBM 1401 mainframe at the Computer History Museum. The program prints a famous line-printer image on the vintage machine. The video is part retro-computing demo and part joke.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as a fun, well-known example of line-printer art on early IBM hardware, and to the channel for vintage computing history.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"vintage computing\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"ibm 1401\",\"line printer\",\"mainframe\",\"computer history\",\"retrocomputing\"],\"quote\":\"Marc's videos on restoring some old IBM hardware, that includes a good bit of the history that went into them\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"Lu56xVlZ40M\",\"title\":\"OpenAI Plays Hide and Seek…and Breaks The Game! 🤖\",\"channel\":\"Two Minute Papers\",\"summary\":\"Two Minute Papers covers OpenAI's multi-agent hide-and-seek experiment, in which agents learned emergent tool use and found ways to break the game's rules. It summarizes the 'Emergent Tool Use from Multi-Agent Interaction' paper.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN mentions it as a fun and slightly worrying example of AI systems behaving in unintended ways when optimizing.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"multi-agent reinforcement learning\",\"categories\":[\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"openai\",\"hide and seek\",\"emergent behavior\",\"reinforcement learning\",\"two minute papers\"],\"quote\":\"OpenAI's hide and seek is one of my favorites\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"M2fKMP47slQ\",\"title\":\"C++Now 2018: You Can Do Better than std::unordered_map: New Improvements to Hash Table Performance\",\"channel\":\"CppNow\",\"summary\":\"Malte Skarupke's C++Now 2018 talk on hash table performance, explaining why std::unordered_map is slow and memory hungry and presenting faster designs, including his bytell hash map.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as a thorough, well-regarded explanation of hash table design tradeoffs and why the standard container is a poor choice.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"hash table performance\",\"categories\":[\"Software performance & optimization\",\"Algorithms & data structures\",\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"hash tables\",\"unordered_map\",\"data structures\",\"performance\"],\"quote\":\"Many videos on hash table are great. I found this one particularly good.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MEqxaH47DTs\",\"title\":\"Is A Toyota SUV \\\"Greener\\\" Than A Tesla?\",\"channel\":\"Engineering Explained\",\"summary\":\"Engineering Explained compares the lifetime CO2 emissions of a Toyota RAV4 Prime plug-in hybrid with a Tesla Model Y, looking at break-even mileage and whether electric cars are worse for the environment.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it for reasonable estimates comparing the emissions of EVs, hybrids and ICE cars.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ev versus hybrid emissions\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"tesla\",\"rav4 prime\",\"co2 emissions\",\"electric vehicles\",\"lifecycle analysis\"],\"quote\":\"I like this youtube video that goes over various crossover points.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0}",
    ",{\"vid\":\"MGMoRu5yrVc\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \\\"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part II\\\"\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"A CppCon 2014 talk by Microsoft engineers on how Office delivers a single-source C++ codebase across iOS, Android, Windows and Mac. It covers architectures, techniques and lessons from shipping cross-platform products.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in Electron, Flutter and React Native debates as evidence that large products can share C++ core code with native views.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"cross-platform c++\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software architecture & design\"],\"tags\":[\"c++\",\"cross-platform\",\"microsoft office\",\"mobile\",\"architecture\"],\"quote\":\"Microsoft have a very interesting approach with their Office suite when it comes to cross-platform development.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MGfhQTbcqmA\",\"title\":\"Neuroscience of Personality | Dario Nardi | Talks at Google\",\"channel\":\"Talks at Google\",\"summary\":\"UCLA professor Dario Nardi presents brain-imaging research suggesting that different Myers-Briggs personality types use their brains in different ways. He discusses how each type can reach a state of creative flow. It is aimed at a general audience.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in MBTI threads as an attempt to link personality types to measurable brain activity patterns.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"neuroscience of personality\",\"categories\":[\"Neuroscience & the brain\",\"Psychology & human behavior\"],\"tags\":[\"myers-briggs\",\"eeg\",\"brain imaging\",\"creativity\",\"jungian functions\"],\"quote\":\"This talk goes into some detail about how specific brain patterns seem to correlate with the Jungian cognitive functions\",\"recommenders\":1,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MNXypBxNGMo\",\"title\":\"Zarch (1987) Gameplay for BBC Acorn Archimedes High Quality\",\"channel\":\"Phoenix Risen\",\"summary\":\"Gameplay footage of Zarch, the 1987 3D Defender-like game by David Braben for the Acorn Archimedes. It shows the 256-colour 3D landscape where you stop landers spreading a virus.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN commenters recall it as a mind-blowing early 3D game that showed what the Archimedes hardware could do.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"early 3d games\",\"categories\":[\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\"],\"tags\":[\"zarch\",\"acorn archimedes\",\"david braben\",\"retro gaming\",\"arm\",\"1987\"],\"quote\":\"It's an awesome 3D game from 1987 (with truly awful sound).\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MO8hZlgK5zc\",\"title\":\"The economics of open source by C J Silverio | JSConf EU 2019\",\"channel\":\"JSConf\",\"summary\":\"C J Silverio, former npm CTO, explains how the JavaScript package commons ended up in the hands of a for-profit company with no accountability. She discusses the cost of running a registry and what the community should do next.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it as the best account of what is wrong with npm's governance and why alternative or decentralized registries are hard.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"package registry economics\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Web development\"],\"tags\":[\"npm\",\"open source sustainability\",\"javascript\",\"package management\",\"decentralized registry\"],\"quote\":\"For those wondering what's wrong with npm, I can recommend this C J Silverio talk\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MSZgoFyuHC8\",\"title\":\"Honest Government Ad | Carbon Capture & Storage\",\"channel\":\"thejuicemedia\",\"summary\":\"A satirical 'Honest Government Ad' from The Juice Media that mocks Australian carbon capture and storage policy. It lays out the fossil fuel industry's influence and the weak results of the technology in a comedic format.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it as a funny but fact-based critique of carbon capture in climate and energy threads.\",\"content_type\":\"comedy\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"carbon capture satire\",\"categories\":[\"Satire & parody\",\"Climate policy, solutions & debate\"],\"tags\":[\"carbon capture\",\"satire\",\"australia\",\"climate policy\",\"fossil fuels\"],\"quote\":\"Comical, but fact-based take\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MVEKt_H3FsI\",\"title\":\"Oral History of Gary Davidian, part 2 of 2\",\"channel\":\"Computer History Museum\",\"summary\":\"Part two of a Computer History Museum oral history with Gary Davidian, covering his work on the 68000 emulator for PowerPC at Apple, Power Computing, clean-room contracting and later performance optimization work. It is nearly three hours long.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as a primary-source interview on Apple's architecture transitions, emulation and microcode work.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"computer history\",\"categories\":[\"History of computing\",\"Compilers, interpreters & program analysis\",\"Computer architecture & CPUs\"],\"tags\":[\"apple\",\"powerpc\",\"68k emulation\",\"microcode\",\"oral history\"],\"quote\":\"There’s an interview with him in the Computer History Museum archive.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MWsGBRdK2N0\",\"title\":\"Suburbs that don't Suck - Streetcar Suburbs (Riverdale, Toronto)\",\"channel\":\"Not Just Bikes\",\"summary\":\"Not Just Bikes explains that car-dependent suburbs are not the only option, using Toronto's Riverdale as an example of a pre-war streetcar suburb. These walkable neighborhoods are popular but are now effectively illegal to build.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it in zoning, density and housing threads as an illustration of the missing middle between city center and car-dependent sprawl.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"streetcar suburbs and urban planning\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\"],\"tags\":[\"urban planning\",\"streetcar suburbs\",\"zoning\",\"walkability\",\"missing middle\",\"toronto\"],\"quote\":\"There is something in between NYC and an American-style suburb. I encourage you to take a look at Not Just Bikes\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"MejbOFk7H6c\",\"title\":\"OK Go - Needing/Getting - Official Video\",\"channel\":\"OK Go\",\"summary\":\"OK Go's music video for Needing/Getting, shot in the desert with a Chevy Sonic fitted with pneumatic arms that play over a thousand instruments across two miles.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an example of inventive, physical-effects music video making, with the car as an instrument.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"music video\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"ok go\",\"music video\",\"practical effects\",\"chevrolet\",\"creative\"],\"quote\":\"4.2s real-time scene with the footage slowed down to 4 minutes\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"Mjx3S3UjmnA\",\"title\":\"CBTC: Communications-Based Train Control\",\"channel\":\"mtainfo\",\"summary\":\"An MTA video on communications-based train control, the system meant to replace the New York City subway's century-old signaling. It also shows the mechanical interlocks and signals in use today.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited as a view into how outdated and error-prone NYC subway signaling is, and how antique interlocking systems work.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"train signaling\",\"categories\":[\"Transit, cities & urban planning\",\"Mechanical & precision engineering\"],\"tags\":[\"cbtc\",\"nyc subway\",\"railway signaling\",\"interlocking\",\"mta\"],\"quote\":\"Well made video that helps understand the interlocks - if you like antique systems!\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{