ack[0].\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OMi6xgdSbMA\",\"title\":\"How Localizing Return of the Obra Dinn Nearly Sunk the Game | War Stories | Ars Technica\",\"channel\":\"Ars Technica\",\"summary\":\"Lucas Pope, creator of Return of the Obra Dinn, explains in Ars Technica's War Stories how localizing the game's logbook nearly sank the project.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended for its insight into game design and cited as a vivid example of how tricky localization can be.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"game localization\",\"categories\":[\"Indie game development\",\"Game design\"],\"tags\":[\"lucas pope\",\"obra dinn\",\"localization\",\"i18n\",\"game development\"],\"quote\":\"combining one of my fave video series with one of my fave gamedevs.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OOK5xkFijPc\",\"title\":\"Power is not energy: why the difference matters\",\"channel\":\"Technology Connections\",\"summary\":\"Technology Connections explains the difference between power (watts) and energy (watt-hours) and why confusing them leads to errors. It runs about 52 minutes and uses everyday examples.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it whenever a thread mixes up power and energy, such as battery specs and solar generation.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"power vs energy\",\"categories\":[\"Power grid, heating & buildings\",\"Fundamental physics\"],\"tags\":[\"watts\",\"energy\",\"electricity\",\"units\",\"physics\"],\"quote\":\"Technology Connections made a pretty good 52 minute video about it the other month\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OOeBJ8mDr8Q\",\"title\":\"Copper vs Pex vs SharkBite - Freeze Testing\",\"channel\":\"Matt Risinger\",\"summary\":\"Builder Matt Risinger freezes water-filled copper and PEX piping capped with SharkBite fittings to see which survives. The video compares how each pipe type handles freezing.\",\"why_hn\":\"Recommended as an interesting experiment showing PEX tolerates freezing better than copper.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"plumbing freeze testing\",\"categories\":[\"Home, DIY & practical skills\",\"Everyday physics & science demos\"],\"tags\":[\"pex\",\"copper pipe\",\"sharkbite\",\"home building\",\"plumbing\"],\"quote\":\"did a freeze comparison between copper, pex, and sharkbite fittings some time back. It was pretty interesting.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OTVE5iPMKLg\",\"title\":\"Your Coffee Addiction Is Probably Good for You\",\"channel\":\"CGP Grey\",\"summary\":\"CGP Grey's short explainer arguing that caffeine addiction is mostly harmless and may even be beneficial. It covers what caffeine does in the body.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN links it in caffeine threads as a quick, clear explanation of caffeine's effects.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"caffeine\",\"categories\":[\"Nutrition & diet science\",\"Neuroscience & the brain\"],\"tags\":[\"coffee\",\"caffeine\",\"addiction\",\"health\",\"cgp grey\"],\"quote\":\"Nice explanatory video on the matter\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OWJCfOvochA\",\"title\":\"Quantum Computing Expert Explains One Concept in 5 Levels of Difficulty | WIRED\",\"channel\":\"WIRED\",\"summary\":\"IBM's Dr. Talia Gershon explains quantum computing to five people of increasing expertise: a child, teen, college student, grad student and professional. The WIRED series format moves from simple analogies to technical detail.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it both as an approachable quantum computing intro and as an example of explaining one concept at multiple levels.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"quantum computing\",\"categories\":[\"Quantum computing\"],\"tags\":[\"quantum computing\",\"wired 5 levels\",\"ibm\",\"qubits\",\"science communication\"],\"quote\":\"I found it easy to comprehend. It starts explaining to a child & works its way up to a doctorate level.\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"ObiqJzfyACM\",\"title\":\"Math Magic\",\"channel\":\"Vsauce\",\"summary\":\"Vsauce explores math through card tricks and big numbers, including an animation of how enormous 52 factorial, the number of deck orderings, really is.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited for its well-known attempt to convey the size of 52 factorial.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"large numbers and combinatorics\",\"categories\":[\"Recreational & visual math\",\"Probability & game theory\",\"Magic, puzzles & curiosities\"],\"tags\":[\"52 factorial\",\"card shuffling\",\"math magic\",\"permutations\",\"vsauce\"],\"quote\":\"Vsauce has a fantastic animation of this too\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OgRFOjVzvm0\",\"title\":\"SICP: o fim de uma era\",\"channel\":\"Computação Raiz\",\"summary\":\"A short Portuguese-language excerpt of Gerald Sussman's 2016 NYC Lisp Meetup talk explaining why MIT replaced the Scheme-based 6.001 (SICP) course with Python courses. The full talk is linked in the description.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it as the canonical explanation from Sussman himself of why SICP ended at MIT.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"sicp and mit curriculum\",\"categories\":[\"Functional programming\",\"Education & learning\"],\"tags\":[\"sicp\",\"sussman\",\"scheme\",\"mit 6.001\",\"python\"],\"quote\":\"we can't mention SICP without referencing this (relatively recent) video\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"OkEdyq3UE5M\",\"title\":\"Starship Troopers - re:View\",\"channel\":\"RedLetterMedia\",\"summary\":\"Mike and Jay of RedLetterMedia discuss Starship Troopers, covering its legacy, why people like or dislike it, and its reading as sci-fi action satire versus the Heinlein book.\",\"why_hn\":\"Pointed to as a good analysis of the film's satire and layers when Starship Troopers or Heinlein come up