s coins the term PDE (Personalized Development Environment) to describe editors like Neovim that you shape to your own workflow. He contrasts this with out-of-the-box IDEs and discusses why customizable tools appeal to developers.\",\"why_hn\":\"It is the cited origin of the 'PDE' term, which HN uses when discussing vim, Neovim and Emacs as personally customized environments.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"personalized development environments\",\"categories\":[\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"neovim\",\"pde\",\"editors\",\"customization\",\"vim\"],\"quote\":\"For anyone not understanding why (neo)vim exists, or why some developers prefer it to IDEs, I suggest watching\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"QUcTsFe1PVs\",\"title\":\"The Epic Of Gilgamesh In Sumerian\",\"channel\":\"Peter Pringle\",\"summary\":\"Peter Pringle sings the opening of the Epic of Gilgamesh in Sumerian, accompanied by a reconstructed ancient instrument. The description gives background on Sumer, cuneiform and how little is known about the language's pronunciation.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it to fans of ancient music as a rare chance to hear an attempt at 4,000-year-old song.\",\"content_type\":\"music\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ancient sumerian music\",\"categories\":[\"Music performance & songs\",\"World history\",\"Language & linguistics\"],\"tags\":[\"gilgamesh\",\"sumerian\",\"ancient music\",\"reconstructed instruments\",\"peter pringle\"],\"quote\":\"If you like this video, you will probably also like Peter Pringle's The Epic Of Gilgamesh In Sumerian\",\"recommenders\":3,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"QWWgr2rN45o\",\"title\":\"Geoffrey Hinton in conversation with Fei-Fei Li — Responsible AI development\",\"channel\":\"Arts & Science - University of Toronto\",\"summary\":\"A 2023 University of Toronto conversation between Geoffrey Hinton and Fei-Fei Li on responsible AI development. They look back at the history of deep learning and ImageNet and discuss the ethics and risks of AI.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends it for Hinton's research history and the two pioneers reflecting on a decade of progress.\",\"content_type\":\"interview\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"ai ethics and history\",\"categories\":[\"AI safety, AGI & impact\",\"Machine learning & deep learning\"],\"tags\":[\"geoffrey hinton\",\"fei-fei li\",\"deep learning\",\"imagenet\",\"responsible ai\"],\"quote\":\"Goes over Hinton's history and why he went the direction he did with his research\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"Qaq9vlfoGnA\",\"title\":\"How a Laser-Shot Disk Defeats Hackers & Pirates (Coding Secrets)\",\"channel\":\"GameHut\",\"summary\":\"An episode of Coding Secrets by Jon Burton on retro copy protection, in particular physically damaging a disk with a laser so copies fail. It explains how this defeated hackers and pirates.\",\"why_hn\":\"Cited in anti-piracy discussions as a clear explanation of clever retro copy-protection tricks, though one commenter disputes a claim about Leander.\",\"content_type\":\"explainer\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"retro copy protection\",\"categories\":[\"Demoscene & retrocomputing\",\"Game history & retro gaming\",\"Speedrunning, glitches & game hacking\"],\"tags\":[\"copy protection\",\"amiga\",\"game development\",\"floppy disks\",\"piracy\"],\"quote\":\"I thought the two methods described in this video were both good\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"QdzV04T_kec\",\"title\":\"AWS re:Invent 2018: [REPEAT 1] A Serverless Journey: AWS Lambda Under the Hood (SRV409-R1)\",\"channel\":\"Amazon Web Services\",\"summary\":\"An AWS re:Invent 2018 session in which Holly Mesrobian and Marc Brooker explain how AWS Lambda is built, including its sandboxing, isolation and scaling architecture. It covers what happens under the hood when serverless code runs.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN cites it as the authoritative description of Lambda internals and isolation, by the engineers who built it.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"aws lambda internals\",\"categories\":[\"Cloud, DevOps & reliability\",\"Distributed systems\"],\"tags\":[\"aws lambda\",\"serverless\",\"isolation\",\"firecracker\",\"reinvent\"],\"quote\":\"You can watch this talk to learn more\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"QeLu_yyz3tc\",\"title\":\"George Friedman, \\\"Europe: Destined for Conflict?\\\"\",\"channel\":\"Chicago Council on Global Affairs\",\"summary\":\"George Friedman of Stratfor speaks at the Chicago Council on Global Affairs about flashpoints in Europe that he expects to erupt again, including borderlands and cultural fault lines.\",\"why_hn\":\"Some recommend it for his geopolitical framing, including a prediction of the Russia-Ukraine conflict, while others found his candor about US policy shocking.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"european geopolitics\",\"categories\":[\"Geopolitics & foreign policy\"],\"tags\":[\"george friedman\",\"stratfor\",\"europe\",\"geopolitics\",\"russia\"],\"quote\":\"I recommend to watch George Friedman's talk\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"QjJaFG63Hlo\",\"title\":\"Seminar with Alan Kay on Object Oriented Programming (VPRI 0246)\",\"channel\":\"Yoshiki Ohshima (大島芳樹)\",\"summary\":\"A long recorded seminar in which Alan Kay discusses object-oriented programming, probably from the mid-1980s. He covers the original idea of objects, messaging and the work done at Xerox.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN points to it as primary-source evidence of what Kay meant by objects, which resembled services and message passing rather than modern class-based OOP.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"primary_topic\":\"object-oriented programming\",\"categories\":[\"Software architecture & design\",\"Tech pioneers & interviews\",\"History of computing\"],\"tags\":[\"alan kay\",\"smal