sequences at a previous job. It is a cautionary tale about irreversible operations.\",\"why_hn\":\"Shared in outage and accidental-deletion threads as a relatable, well-told horror story.\",\"content_type\":\"commentary\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"programming mistakes\",\"categories\":[\"Software practice, open source & careers\",\"Databases & storage\",\"Testing, debugging & developer tools\"],\"tags\":[\"tom scott\",\"sql\",\"data loss\",\"postmortem\",\"human error\"],\"quote\":\"Tom Scott has a great video on his \\\"Oh Shoot\\\" moment\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"X8aWBcPVPMo\",\"title\":\"Richard Feynman on hungry philosophers (or do we see objects or only their light)\",\"channel\":\"Zero\",\"summary\":\"A one-minute clip from Richard Feynman's 1979 Auckland lecture in which he answers whether you see an object or only its light. It is a short, playful take on perception and on the use of philosophy.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN uses it to illustrate Feynman's wry view of philosophical questions and perception.\",\"content_type\":\"lecture\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"perception and philosophy\",\"categories\":[\"Philosophy & ethics\",\"Fundamental physics\",\"Scientific method & science communication\"],\"tags\":[\"feynman\",\"light\",\"perception\",\"philosophy of science\"],\"quote\":\"A cute video of Feynman answering the question of whether you're seeing an object, or only the light from an object, when looking at it\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"XO9FKQAxWZc\",\"title\":\"Copyright is Brain Damage | Nina Paley | TEDxMaastricht\",\"channel\":\"TEDx Talks\",\"summary\":\"Nina Paley's TEDxMaastricht talk arguing against copyright and for copyleft and free culture. She argues that restricting ideas harms creativity and that culture belongs to everyone.\",\"why_hn\":\"Brought up in copyright, public domain and AI-training debates as a provocative argument for abolishing copyright.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"copyright and free culture\",\"categories\":[\"Law, policing & justice\",\"Hacker culture & free software\",\"Free speech, censorship & civil liberties\"],\"tags\":[\"nina paley\",\"copyleft\",\"public domain\",\"intellectual property\",\"tedx\"],\"quote\":\"had a great talk five years ago on how restricting information is a form of cultural cancer\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"XRAP3lBivYM\",\"title\":\"CppCon 2019: Emery Berger “Mesh: Automatically Compacting Your C++ Application's Memory”\",\"channel\":\"CppCon\",\"summary\":\"Emery Berger's CppCon 2019 talk introducing Mesh, a drop-in malloc replacement that compacts memory in unmodified C and C++ programs. It tackles fragmentation by merging pages without changing pointers.\",\"why_hn\":\"HN recommends Berger's talks as fascinating and cites Mesh as mind-blowing memory allocator work.\",\"content_type\":\"talk\",\"level\":\"advanced\",\"primary_topic\":\"memory allocation\",\"categories\":[\"C, C++, Rust, Go & systems languages\",\"Software performance & optimization\"],\"tags\":[\"mesh\",\"malloc\",\"memory fragmentation\",\"compaction\",\"c++\"],\"quote\":\"the weird and wonderful work of Emery Berger et al with their “Mesh” malloc implementation, which blows my mind\",\"recommenders\":2,\"score\":2.0},{\"vid\":\"XRXwWbo_mX0\",\"title\":\"Renault F1 engine playing God Save the Queen\",\"channel\":\"Chris Billett\",\"summary\":\"A short clip of a Renault F1 engine playing God Save the Queen by precisely controlling its RPM, recorded at Goodwood. It demonstrates how finely the drivetrain can be controlled.\",\"why_hn\":\"Treated as a favorite hack and a go-to example in threads about machines playing music.\",\"content_type\":\"demo\",\"level\":\"general\",\"primary_topic\":\"engine rpm music\",\"categories\":[\"Cars, EVs & motorcycles\",\"Music performance & songs\"],\"tags\":[\"formula one\",\"renault\",\"hack\",\"goodwood\",\"engine control\"],\"q